@host = http://localhost:5227

# =============================================================================
# GET /api/lenses
#
# No parameters, no body, always returns 200.
# Returns the lenses from calibration.json, each with its id, name and three
# weights. Its purpose: the client learns which lens ids are valid before it
# POSTs. The id is what gets stored and sent; the name is only ever displayed.
# =============================================================================

### The lenses the onboarding screen offers (Story 2.2)
GET {{host}}/api/lenses
Accept: application/json

### A route that does not exist — must return 404
GET {{host}}/api/lens
Accept: application/json

### The OpenAPI description of the API (Development only)
GET {{host}}/openapi/v1.json
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# POST /api/grades
#
# Send a food's nutrients per 100 g plus a lens name, get back the letter.
# No database, no auth: every request recomputes everything.
#
# The request passes four filters, in order. The first one that fails stops
# everything and returns 400:
#
#   1. The annotations on GradeRequestDto, run by [ApiController] BEFORE the
#      action. A missing required field or a negative value means the action
#      never runs. The nutrients are double? precisely so that an absent field
#      does not silently become 0.
#   2. The lens id, matched case-insensitively. Not found → 400 through
#      ValidationProblem, so it has exactly the same shape as the automatic
#      errors. lensId is the stable slug ("weight-loss"), never the display
#      name: renaming or translating a lens must not break saved references.
#   3. NutrientPlausibility.Check — energy against the macronutrients. Two ways
#      to fail: energy too high for any food (kilojoules sent as kilocalories),
#      or energy that does not follow from the protein, fat and carbohydrate.
#      This is why carbohydrate is required even though the engine never
#      scores it.
#   4. The engine: ScoreCombiner.Combine produces the score,
#      Calibration.GradeFor turns it into the letter.
#
# In the response:
#   grade: null       — a nutritionally empty food (water). NOT a bad grade;
#                       the product shows no letter at all.
#   isPartial /       — the engine worked with incomplete data: the component
#   isEstimated         was estimated, not measured.
#   the hidden rule   — a MEASURED density below the floor, on a food whose
#                       category has no rule → E, whatever the score is.
# =============================================================================

### Chicken breast under Weight Loss — 200, grade A
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "weight-loss",
  "calories": 165, "protein": 31, "fat": 3.6, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 1.0, "sodium": 74, "carbohydrate": 0,
  "potassium": 256, "magnesium": 29, "iron": 0.7, "calcium": 15, "vitaminC": 0
}

### The same food, a different lens — a different letter
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "fitness",
  "calories": 165, "protein": 31, "fat": 3.6, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 1.0, "sodium": 74, "carbohydrate": 0
}

### Filter 2 — a lens id that does not exist — 400, pointing at GET /api/lenses
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "keto",
  "calories": 165, "protein": 31, "fat": 3.6, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 1, "sodium": 74, "carbohydrate": 0
}

### Filter 1 — missing fields and a negative value — 400 with every error at once
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{ "lensId": "fitness", "calories": 165, "protein": -5 }

### Filter 3 — kilojoules sent as if they were kilocalories — 400
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "fitness",
  "calories": 690, "protein": 31, "fat": 3.6, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 1, "sodium": 74, "carbohydrate": 0
}

### Water — 200, but grade: null. No letter, not a bad letter.
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "weight-loss",
  "calories": 0, "protein": 0, "fat": 0, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 0, "sodium": 4, "carbohydrate": 0
}

# =============================================================================
# GET /api/foods
#
# The catalogue: 1 933 foods loaded from FNDDS. Four optional parameters,
# all of them combinable:
#
#   search    — case-insensitive "contains" on the description, run by
#               PostgreSQL as ILIKE
#   category  — exact match on the FNDDS category, as stored. Spaces must be
#               written %20: a raw space ends the URL in the request line
#   page      — 1-based. Out of range is not an error: items comes back empty
#   pageSize  — 25 by default, 100 at most. Above that: 400
#
# The response is an envelope, not a bare array: items carries the page,
# total carries how many rows match the filters before paging cuts them.
#
# A list row carries id, description and category only. The sixteen nutrients
# never leave the database for a list — the projection asks PostgreSQL for
# three columns.
#
# Ordering is by description, always. Without it PostgreSQL owes no promise
# that two requests return rows in the same order, and page 2 could repeat or
# skip what page 1 showed.
# =============================================================================

### The first page, no filters — 25 of 1 933
GET {{host}}/api/foods
Accept: application/json

### Everything with milk in the name — total must be 93
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=milk
Accept: application/json

### The same search shouted — ILIKE ignores case, total stays 93
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=MILK
Accept: application/json

### One category, exactly as stored — total must be 160
GET {{host}}/api/foods?category=Chicken,%20whole%20pieces
Accept: application/json

### Search and category together — both filters apply
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=roasted&category=Chicken,%20whole%20pieces
Accept: application/json

### Page 2 of the milks, five at a time
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=milk&page=2&pageSize=5
Accept: application/json

### Past the last page — 200 with an empty items, not 404
GET {{host}}/api/foods?page=500
Accept: application/json

### Over the ceiling — 400, the [Range] attribute stops it before the action
GET {{host}}/api/foods?pageSize=500
Accept: application/json

### Page zero — 400, pages are 1-based
GET {{host}}/api/foods?page=0
Accept: application/json
# =============================================================================
# GET /api/foods/{id}
#
# One food, with the sixteen nutrients the list leaves out. The id is the
# database key, stable for the life of the row (ADR 0006) — safe to store in a
# URL, a log entry or a foreign key.
#
# Ids in the loaded catalogue run from 5347 to 7279. They do not start at 1:
# the identity sequence kept counting through the reloads that happened before
# 0006 stopped them.
#
#   200  the food, as an object
#   404  no row with that id, AND anything that is not a number — the {id:int}
#        route constraint means /api/foods/milk matches no route at all, so it
#        never reaches the action and never becomes a 400
#
# fdcId is provenance: the USDA row these numbers came from. It is null for a
# food typed in by hand, which is not the same as "no source".
#
# A nutrient is null when the catalogue has no value for it, which is not zero.
# leucine is null on all 1 933 rows — the engine derives it from the category
# instead of reading it here.
# =============================================================================

### Whole milk by id — 200, sixteen nutrients, fdcId 2705385, leucine null
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5348
Accept: application/json

### The first row of the catalogue — id 5347, not 1
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5347
Accept: application/json

### The last row — id 7279
GET {{host}}/api/foods/7279
Accept: application/json

### One past the last id — 404, not an empty object
GET {{host}}/api/foods/7280
Accept: application/json

### An id below the range the sequence ever reached — 404
GET {{host}}/api/foods/1
Accept: application/json

### Not a number — 404 from the route constraint, before any action runs
GET {{host}}/api/foods/milk
Accept: application/json

### Find it in the list first, then open it — the id in items is this id
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=broccoli,%20raw
Accept: application/json

### The row the search above returns — 200
GET {{host}}/api/foods/6626
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# GET /api/foods/{id}/grade?lensId=…
#
# The letter of a catalogue food. The database supplies the food, the engine
# supplies the letter, and nobody sends nutrients. The bridge between them is
# ScoringInput.From, in Sated.Services.
#
# The response has EXACTLY the shape of POST /api/grades, so a client can
# handle both the same way.
#
#   200  the letter plus the four components
#   400  lensId missing or unknown — the same ProblemDetails shape as
#        POST /api/grades, with the error on the lensId field
#   404  no food with that id, and any id that is not a number
#
# What shows up here and nowhere else:
#   proteinQuality.isEstimated is ALWAYS true for a catalogue food. FNDDS
#   carries no leucine, so the engine estimates it from protein and category.
#   grade: null means "nothing to grade", never "a bad grade".
# =============================================================================

### Whole milk under Weight Loss — 200, grade B
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5348/grade?lensId=weight-loss
Accept: application/json

### The same milk under Fitness — 200, grade C. Another goal, another letter.
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5348/grade?lensId=fitness
Accept: application/json

### And under GLP-1 — 200, B again, but a different score
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5348/grade?lensId=glp-1
Accept: application/json

### Tap water — 200, but grade: null and density: null
GET {{host}}/api/foods/7242/grade?lensId=weight-loss
Accept: application/json

### A lens that does not exist — 400, the error sits on lensId
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5348/grade?lensId=keto
Accept: application/json

### No lensId at all — 400, treated exactly like an unknown lens
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5348/grade
Accept: application/json

### A food that is not there — 404
GET {{host}}/api/foods/999999/grade?lensId=fitness
Accept: application/json

### An id that is not a number — 404 from the route constraint
GET {{host}}/api/foods/milk/grade?lensId=fitness
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# GET /api/foods/{id}/grades
#
# The same food under every lens at once, so a client can show the comparison
# without switching the user's active lens (Story 5.3). Plural, and it takes no
# lensId: asking for all of them is the whole point.
#
#   200  one entry per lens, in the order GET /api/lenses uses
#   404  no food with that id, and any id that is not a number
#
# Each entry nests the response of GET /api/foods/{id}/grade unchanged, so the
# letter reads grade.grade. The outer object is the lens, the inner one is the
# grade under it.
#
# Measured over the whole catalogue: 71.6% of foods change letter between
# lenses, so the comparison has something to show. Weight Loss and Fitness
# disagree on 69.8% of foods; GLP-1 and Weight Loss on 8.5%, because those two
# carry the same weights and differ only in which nutrients density counts.
#
# No cookie needed. The public food page of Story 9.1 calls this endpoint.
# =============================================================================

### Whole milk under all three lenses — 200, B / C / B in that order
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5348/grades
Accept: application/json

### Tap water — 200, three entries, every one with grade: null
GET {{host}}/api/foods/7242/grades
Accept: application/json

### A food that is not there — 404, not an empty list
GET {{host}}/api/foods/999999/grades
Accept: application/json

### An id that is not a number — 404 from the route constraint
GET {{host}}/api/foods/milk/grades
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# GET /api/foods/{id}/swap?lensId=…
#
# Three foods from the same category that carry a better letter under that lens
# (Story 6.1). Only on request: nothing else in the API returns a swap.
#
#   200  up to three alternatives, best score first
#   400  lensId missing or unknown — same shape as /grade
#   404  no food with that id, and any id that is not a number
#
# The candidates must beat the food on the LETTER, and are ranked among
# themselves by SCORE, with the lower id winning an exact tie. Measured over the
# catalogue: 44.0% of foods have no better letter in their category at all, and
# that answer is a 200 with an empty list and the message, never a 404.
#
# Only catalogue foods are ever suggested, so a signed-in caller and a stranger
# get the same three. A food you entered yourself is not offered back to you.
# =============================================================================

### Canned peach in syrup (B) — 200, three A's: nectarine, juice pack, raw peach
GET {{host}}/api/foods/6416/swap?lensId=weight-loss
Accept: application/json

### The same peach under Fitness — 200, and only ONE alternative, a C
GET {{host}}/api/foods/6416/swap?lensId=fitness
Accept: application/json

### Raw nectarine, the best of its category — 200, empty list plus the message
GET {{host}}/api/foods/6410/swap?lensId=weight-loss
Accept: application/json

### Tap water, which has no letter — 200, empty, nothing can be better than none
GET {{host}}/api/foods/7242/swap?lensId=weight-loss
Accept: application/json

### A lens that does not exist — 400, the error sits on lensId
GET {{host}}/api/foods/6416/swap?lensId=keto
Accept: application/json

### A food that is not there — 404
GET {{host}}/api/foods/999999/swap?lensId=weight-loss
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# /api/auth  —  register, login, logout, me
#
# THESE REQUESTS NEED HTTPS. The session cookie is marked Secure, so a client
# talking to {{host}} over plain http receives the Set-Cookie header and throws
# it away — register would answer 200 and the next request would still be 401.
# Run the API with the https profile: dotnet run --launch-profile https
#
# There is no token to copy anywhere. The client sends the cookie by itself
# after register or login, and stops sending it after logout.
#
# Password rules, from Program.cs: at least 12 characters, and nothing else.
# No digit, no capital, no symbol — length is what is asked for. See
# 02_planning/6.prd-addendum-account-security §3.2 in the private docs.
#
# A failed login answers the same way whether the email has an account or the
# password is wrong. Registration is the deliberate exception: it has to say
# that the address is taken, or nobody could tell why they cannot sign up.
# =============================================================================

@secureHost = https://localhost:7245
@email = florin@sated.test
@password = abcdefghijkl

### Register — 200, returns id and email, and sets the sated.session cookie
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Who am I — 200 with the same id, because register already signed you in
GET {{secureHost}}/api/auth/me
Accept: application/json

### Sign out — 204, and the cookie is cleared
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/logout

### Who am I now — 401, and no Location header pointing anywhere
GET {{secureHost}}/api/auth/me
Accept: application/json

### Sign back in — 200, a fresh cookie
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### The same email twice — 400, DuplicateEmail and DuplicateUserName
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Eleven characters — 400, PasswordTooShort
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "eleven@sated.test",
  "password": "abcdefghijk"
}

### Twelve lowercase letters, no digit or symbol — 200, this is allowed on purpose
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "twelve@sated.test",
  "password": "abcdefghijkl"
}

### Not an email — 400 from [ApiController], before the action runs
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "florin",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Wrong password — 401, body says only Unauthorized
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "wrongbutlongenough"
}

### An email with no account — 401, byte for byte the same answer as above
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nobody@sated.test",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

# Send the one above five times, then the RIGHT password below — still 401,
# because the account is locked for five minutes. That is the only way to see it.
### After five wrong attempts, the right password — 401, the account is locked
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### More than ten logins in a minute from one address — 429, whatever the email
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Logout without a session — 401, not 204
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/logout

# =============================================================================
# /api/consents  and  /api/profile  —  onboarding (Story 2.2)
#
# Same rule as the block above: HTTPS, or the session cookie is thrown away.
# Sign in with the register or login request above before running these.
#
# The order matters, and it is the point of the story. Weight is health data,
# so PUT /api/profile refuses with 403 until a consent exists. Consent is its
# own request against its own resource — it is never a field inside the request
# that carries the data, because then it would not be a separate act.
#
# Consent is a document plus a signature, not a flag. GET returns the text in
# force; POST names the version you are signing. A version you never fetched is
# refused, so nobody can consent to a text they were never shown.
#
# Withdrawing deletes what the consent covered — the weight. That is written in
# the text itself, before anyone accepts it.
# =============================================================================

### The consent text in force — 200, with version, full text, and givenAt null
GET {{secureHost}}/api/consents/HealthData
Accept: application/json

### Before consenting — 200, but weightKg and activeLensId are both null
GET {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Accept: application/json

### Onboarding without consent — 403, and nothing is stored
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "weightKg": 82,
  "heightCm": 180,
  "activeLensId": "weight-loss"
}

### A version that was never published — 400, the error sits on version
POST {{secureHost}}/api/consents/HealthData
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "version": "1999-01-01"
}

### Consent to the version GET returned — 200, and givenAt is now set
POST {{secureHost}}/api/consents/HealthData
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "version": "2026-08-31"
}

### The same request again — 200, and givenAt has NOT moved. One signature.
POST {{secureHost}}/api/consents/HealthData
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "version": "2026-08-31"
}

### Onboarding, now allowed — 200 with the profile that was just stored
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "weightKg": 82,
  "heightCm": 180,
  "activeLensId": "weight-loss"
}

### A lens that does not exist — 400, the error sits on activeLensId
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "weightKg": 82,
  "heightCm": 180,
  "activeLensId": "keto"
}

### Ten kilograms — 400 from the range on the DTO, before the action runs
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "weightKg": 10,
  "heightCm": 180,
  "activeLensId": "weight-loss"
}

### No height at all — 400. Both measurements or no target (Story 7.1)
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "weightKg": 82,
  "activeLensId": "weight-loss"
}

### Forty centimetres — 400 from the range, before the action runs
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "weightKg": 82,
  "heightCm": 40,
  "activeLensId": "weight-loss"
}

# =============================================================================
# PUT /api/profile/calorie-target      DELETE /api/profile/calorie-target
#
# Story 7.4 and 7.5, FR-31 and FR-32. The third axis of the Day Ring, and the
# only one the user sets rather than the product deriving.
#
# ITS OWN RESOURCE, not a field in PUT /api/profile. The criterion is that
# onboarding must not ask for it. As a field it would be asked for on every
# profile save, and a client updating only the weight would wipe it — "absent"
# and "null" arrive identically in a double?. Same argument consent gets.
#
# NOTHING derives it. Not from weight, not from age, not from activity. That is
# a requirement to NOT build something, so there is no code to point at.
#
# Below 1,200 kcal the response carries a warning. It does NOT block, does not
# ask for confirmation, and does not repeat itself on the Day Ring afterwards —
# it is returned by the write that caused it, and nowhere else.
# =============================================================================

### A target of 2,000 — 200, stored, no warning
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile/calorie-target
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kcal": 2000
}

### 1,100 — 200 and STORED, with the warning alongside. Not a 400.
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile/calorie-target
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kcal": 1100
}

### Exactly 1,200 — 200, and no warning. The threshold is below, not below-or-equal.
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile/calorie-target
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kcal": 1200
}

### 100 kcal — 400 from the range on the DTO. That is a typo, not a choice.
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile/calorie-target
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kcal": 100
}

### The day now carries the third axis — calories.consumed against calories.targetKcal
GET {{secureHost}}/api/days/2026-08-31
Accept: application/json

### Remove the target — 204
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/profile/calorie-target

### The day again — calories is null now, and NOTHING else moved
# Not an empty object, not a zero target: the axis is absent. Compare protein
# and grade against the request two above — both identical.
GET {{secureHost}}/api/days/2026-08-31
Accept: application/json

### Withdraw — 204. As few steps as giving it was.
# This deletes what the consent document says it deletes: the weight, the HEIGHT,
# and every day logged. Meals, entries and days all go with it, by cascade.
# What stays: the account, the signature with its withdrawnAt, the active lens,
# the calorie target, and any foods or recipes of your own — none of those are
# what the document names.
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/consents/HealthData

### The profile now — 200, weightKg AND heightCm are null again, consent is false
GET {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Accept: application/json

### Withdraw again, with nothing to withdraw — 404
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/consents/HealthData

### Consents without a session — 401
GET {{secureHost}}/api/consents/HealthData
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# POST /api/account/export
# DELETE /api/account
#
# Story 2.3, FR-29. Two endpoints, one rule: both ask for the password again.
# A session cookie on its own opens neither.
#
# Why export is a POST and not a GET: a GET has nowhere to carry a password.
# The export is the whole health file in one document, so it is the request
# worth protecting most — a stolen cookie that could fetch it takes everything
# and leaves no trace. Google Takeout and Facebook re-ask for the same reason.
#
# Wrong passwords count towards the same lockout as a failed login: five, and
# the account is locked for five minutes. A wrong password answers 403, not
# 401 — the caller is known, the request is well formed, permission is what is
# missing. A locked account answers 403 too, and says nothing about being
# locked.
#
# Deletion is final. There is no soft delete, no grace period, no undo.
# =============================================================================

### Sign in, so this section stands on its own — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Consent, so the export has something to carry — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/consents/HealthData
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "version": "2026-08-31"
}

### Onboarding, so the export has a weight and a lens — 200
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "weightKg": 82,
  "heightCm": 180,
  "activeLensId": "weight-loss"
}

### Export with the wrong password — 403, and no data in the body
POST {{secureHost}}/api/account/export
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "not the password"
}

### Export — 200, attachment, with the weight, the lens and every consent text
POST {{secureHost}}/api/account/export
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Delete with the wrong password — 403, and the account is still there
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/account
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "not the password"
}

### The account is still there — 200
GET {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Accept: application/json

### Delete — 204, and Set-Cookie clears sated.session
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/account
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### The session is gone — 401
GET {{secureHost}}/api/profile
Accept: application/json

### The email is free again, so the row really went — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

# =============================================================================
# GET  /api/foods/categories
# POST /api/foods
#
# Story 3.3, FR-11. A food you type in from a package label. It belongs to you
# alone: `Foods.OwnerId` holds your account id, and a global query filter on the
# entity puts "or it is mine" into every query the API makes against that table.
# Nothing in FoodsController mentions the owner.
#
# The body asks for what a nutrition label prints, per 100 g:
#   required  calories · protein · fat · carbohydrate · fibre · saturated fat · sodium
#   optional  vitamin D · calcium · iron · potassium   (the only four labels carry)
#
# Carbohydrate is required and NOT stored. The engine never reads it; it is here
# so NutrientPlausibility can check that the energy follows from the macros.
# Without it, bread reads as four times the energy its macros imply and would be
# rejected as a typo.
#
# Category is required and must be one the catalogue already uses, because the
# name selects the calibrated category rule (FR-6). GET .../categories is the
# list; it is read from the catalogue rows themselves, not from a copy.
# =============================================================================

### Sign in — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### The categories the engine has rules for — 200, an array of names
GET {{secureHost}}/api/foods/categories
Accept: application/json

