# =============================================================================
# Provenance — GET /api/foods and GET /api/foods/{id}
#
# Story 3.2, FR-10. Every food says where its numbers came from. Two places:
#   the list  `source` per row, so your own food is distinguishable from the
#             catalogue's without being marked as worse
#   the detail `provenance` — the row's source, plus the fields that did not
#             come from it
#
# Per-field provenance is DERIVED, not stored. Measured on the real catalogue:
# all 1,933 rows carry all fifteen nutrients and none carries leucine, so there
# is no variation inside a row to store. Sixteen source columns would hold the
# same constant 29,000 times. What is stored is the row's source, because that
# is the part that will vary — see decision 0011 and 0012.
#
#   estimated  the value is absent and the engine fills it in (leucine, from
#              the category)
#   absent     the value is absent and nothing fills it in — the density score
#              renormalises over what is left and reports isEstimated
# =============================================================================

### A catalogue food — 200. source is UsdaFndds, absent empty, leucine estimated.
### 5347 is Milk, NFS; any id from the list above works.
GET {{secureHost}}/api/foods/5347
Accept: application/json

### The list — 200. Every row carries source; nothing else about it changed
GET {{secureHost}}/api/foods?pageSize=3
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# POST   /api/recipes          GET /api/recipes         GET /api/recipes/{id}
# PUT    /api/recipes/{id}     DELETE /api/recipes/{id}
# GET    /api/recipes/{id}/grade?lensId=
#
# Story 3.4, FR-12. A saved composition of foods with weights. Private, like
# your own foods, and behind the same global query filter — a recipe belongs to
# exactly one account and there is no shared catalogue of them.
#
# The nutrient profile is NOT stored. PortionAggregate (Story 1.10) sums the
# nutrients across the portions and restates them per 100 g of total weight,
# every time it is read. A stored profile would drift the moment an ingredient
# changed. The grade is then computed on that profile by the same formula that
# grades a single food — a plate of spinach and butter reads as butter, because
# 97% of its calories are butter, and no average of letters could say that.
#
# Absent stays absent all the way up: one ingredient that does not know its
# vitamin C makes the whole recipe not know it.
#
# Ingredients are in GRAMS only. "2 eggs" needs Food.Servings, which does not
# exist yet, so DisplayAmount/DisplayUnit from the architecture are not here.
#
# NOT built, and it is not an oversight: "adding a recipe to a Meal is one
# action" and "editing a recipe does not rewrite meals already logged" both
# need Meal, which is Epic 4. The second is solved on the Meal side — grams are
# frozen at logging time.
# =============================================================================

### Sign in — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Compose one — 201 + Location. 5347 is Milk, NFS; 5348 is Milk, whole
POST {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Milk and the next thing along",
  "ingredients": [
    { "foodId": 5347, "grams": 200 },
    { "foodId": 5348, "grams": 100 }
  ]
}

### My recipes — 200, each with its ingredient count and total weight
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Accept: application/json

### One recipe — 200 with the derived profile per 100 g of the 300 g total.
### Replace 1 with the id the Location header returned.
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1
Accept: application/json

### Its grade — 200, computed on the aggregate like any single food
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1/grade?lensId=weight-loss
Accept: application/json

### A recipe with no ingredients — 400, the error sits on ingredients
POST {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Nothing at all",
  "ingredients": []
}

### A food that does not exist, or is somebody else's — 400. Same answer for
### both, so this cannot be used to find out whether an id is taken
POST {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Not mine to use",
  "ingredients": [
    { "foodId": 999999, "grams": 100 }
  ]
}

### Edit it — 200, the ingredients are replaced and the profile follows
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Just the milk now",
  "ingredients": [
    { "foodId": 5347, "grams": 250 }
  ]
}

### The export carries recipes too — 200, with every ingredient and its weight
POST {{secureHost}}/api/account/export
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Delete it — 204, and its ingredients go with it
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1

### Gone — 404
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1
Accept: application/json

### Recipes without a session — 401
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# Servings — on GET /api/foods/{id}
#
# Imported from the same FNDDS file the catalogue came from: 6,810 servings
# across all 1,933 foods, five each on average, none without.
#
# Two things live here, and they are not the same thing:
#   servings      the named measures a person can pick — "1 egg", "1 cup"
#   typicalGrams  what USDA assumes was eaten when the survey respondent did
#                 not say. It matches one of the named servings only 40.7% of
#                 the time; the rest of the time it is its own number.
#
# THE TRAP, measured: foodPortions in the USDA file is NOT in order. Taking
# element [0] of the array gives "1 cup (4.86 large eggs) = 243 g" for an egg.
# Sorted by sequenceNumber it gives "1 large = 50 g". Sated sorts.
#
# typicalGrams is inert until FR-14, which needs an amount when the sentence
# did not carry one. It is exposed anyway: a field no test can see is a field
# nobody knows is right.
# =============================================================================

### An egg — 200. servings starts at "1 egg" 50 g, and typicalGrams is 50
GET {{secureHost}}/api/foods/5943
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# POST /api/meals            POST /api/meals/{id}/entries
# GET  /api/meals/{id}       GET  /api/days/{date}
#
# Story 4.1, FR-13. Log a food into a meal, by grams or by one of the servings
# the food carries.
#
# THE DATE COMES FROM THE CLIENT. The server cannot know your time zone, and
# "which day was it" has to be frozen when you log — otherwise a flight rewrites
# your history. It is also what lets you log yesterday's dinner.
#
# Three quantity fields, all three from the architecture:
#   QuantityGrams      the truth for the engine, frozen at logging
#   DisplayAmount +    what you actually said — "2" and "1 egg". Without them,
#   DisplayUnit        you open the entry to edit it and see 100 g, and "2 eggs"
#                      is gone for good
#   QuantityEstimated  false everywhere until FR-14 proposes a quantity
#
# Grams OR a serving, never both and never neither. Both would mean the API
# picks which one you meant.
#
# The grade comes back with the entry, under YOUR active lens — that is what
# "the grade appears immediately, without a further action" means. No active
# lens yet (onboarding unfinished) and the meal still logs; grade is null.
#
# Each meal stamps EngineVersion, from calibration.json. Inert: written, never
# read at render time. Grades always render at the current version.
# =============================================================================

### Sign in — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### A meal on a date — 201 + Location. The Day is created if it is the first
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "date": "2026-08-31",
  "name": "Breakfast"
}

### Two eggs, by serving — 200. Replace 1 with the meal id above.
### quantityGrams comes back 100; displayAmount 2 and displayUnit "1 egg"
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "foodId": 5943,
  "servingCount": 2,
  "servingDescription": "1 egg"
}

### And 150 g of something, by weight — 200, and the grade moves
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "foodId": 5347,
  "grams": 150
}

### Grams AND a serving — 400. The API will not pick which one you meant
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "foodId": 5943,
  "grams": 60,
  "servingCount": 1,
  "servingDescription": "1 egg"
}

### Neither — 400, same reason
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "foodId": 5943
}

### A serving this food does not have — 400 on servingDescription
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "foodId": 5943,
  "servingCount": 1,
  "servingDescription": "1 bucket"
}

### The meal — 200 with its entries and the aggregate grade
GET {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1
Accept: application/json

### The day — 200 with every meal, the protein block and the Day Grade (7.1, 7.2)
# grade is the whole day recomputed as ONE plate: every entry of every meal pooled,
# nutrients summed by grams, renormalised to 100 g, then the formula rerun. It is NOT
# an average of the meals' letters, and not an average of their scores either — see
# decision 0018. A day of one meal is graded byte for byte like that meal.
# protein.grams counts every entry of every meal, at the food's protein per
# 100 g times the grams logged. targetMinGrams/targetMaxGrams come from the
# ADJUSTED weight — ideal weight at BMI 22 plus a quarter of the excess over
# it — times the g/kg range the active lens carries in calibration.json.
# At 82 kg and 180 cm under Weight Loss that is 118.3 to 162.7 g.
GET {{secureHost}}/api/days/2026-08-31
Accept: application/json

### The same day under Fitness — the protein does not move, the target does
# Run the PUT /api/profile above with "fitness" first. Fitness asks 1.4-2.0
# against Weight Loss's 1.6-2.2, so both ends of the target drop and
# protein.grams is byte for byte what it was.
GET {{secureHost}}/api/days/2026-08-31
Accept: application/json

### A day with nothing on it — 200, empty meals, protein.grams 0, target still there
# An empty day is zero protein against a real target, and grade is null — never the
# letter E. A day nobody logged is not a bad day (FR-21).
GET {{secureHost}}/api/days/2020-01-01
Accept: application/json

### The same food split into two meals — same day grade (Story 7.2)
# Log the two POSTs below on 2026-08-30, then read that day. Its grade must equal
# the grade of the single meal above that holds the same two foods: how you group
# your food into meals cannot move the day's letter. That is the whole decision.
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "date": "2026-08-30",
  "name": "Split, first half"
}

### The second half, its own meal
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "date": "2026-08-30",
  "name": "Split, second half"
}

### The split day — grade identical to the day above, protein identical too
GET {{secureHost}}/api/days/2026-08-30
Accept: application/json

### The export carries the meals, with the frozen grams — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/account/export
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Meals without a session — 401
GET {{secureHost}}/api/days/2026-08-31
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# A recipe, logged into a meal — POST /api/meals/{id}/entries with recipeId
#
# The last two criteria of FR-12, which needed Meal to exist.
#
# Logging a recipe UNPACKS it into its foods, then and there. What lands in the
# meal is ordinary entries — food plus grams — with no live link to the recipe.
# That is what makes "editing a recipe does not rewrite meals already logged"
# true by construction rather than by a rule somebody has to remember.
#
# You say how many grams of the recipe you ate; every ingredient is scaled by
# the same share. A 600 g recipe logged at 300 g halves each ingredient.
#
# Each entry carries fromRecipeId and fromRecipeName so a screen can group them
# back into one row. fromRecipeId is NOT a foreign key, on purpose: delete the
# recipe and the meal stands, name and all. It records what happened, the same
# way engineVersion does.
#
# Servings do not apply to a recipe — grams only.
# =============================================================================

### Sign in — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Half of a 600 g recipe — 200, two entries at 100 g and 200 g.
### Replace both 1s with the meal and recipe ids you got above.
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "recipeId": 1,
  "grams": 300
}

### A recipe without grams — 400. Servings belong to a food, not a recipe
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "recipeId": 1
}

### A food AND a recipe in one call — 400
POST {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "foodId": 5347,
  "recipeId": 1,
  "grams": 100
}

### Now edit the recipe out of all recognition — 200
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Something else entirely",
  "ingredients": [
    { "foodId": 5347, "grams": 1000 }
  ]
}

### The meal has not moved — 200, same entries, same grams, old recipe name
GET {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1
Accept: application/json

### Delete the recipe — 204
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1

### The meal still stands — 200, fromRecipeName is still there
GET {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# Editing and deleting — FR-15, Story 4.3
#
# PUT    /api/meals/{id}                     rename it
# DELETE /api/meals/{id}                     remove the whole meal
# PUT    /api/meals/{id}/entries/{entryId}   change one quantity
# DELETE /api/meals/{id}/entries/{entryId}   remove one entry
# DELETE /api/meals/{id}/recipes/{recipeId}  remove a logged recipe, all of it
#
# Everything that changes a meal returns the meal, regraded. Nothing is stored,
# so "the grade recalculates immediately" is not a feature — it is what happens
# when nothing was cached.
#
# ONE THING WORTH KNOWING: changing how much you ate of a SINGLE food does not
# move the meal's grade. The grade is the quality of the mixture per 100 g, and
# 400 g of cheese is the same mixture as 100 g of cheese. Change one food in a
# meal of two and it moves, because the mixture moved. Quantity enters at the
# day level (FR-21), where meals are weighted by how much was eaten.
#
# A logged recipe is several entries, so removing it takes a route of its own.
# Deleting a meal never touches the recipes it was logged from — it could not,
# there is no link to follow.
# =============================================================================

### Sign in — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Change one entry to 400 g — 200 with the meal regraded. Replace the ids.
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "grams": 400
}

### Or into servings — 200. displayAmount and displayUnit change with it
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "servingCount": 3,
  "servingDescription": "1 egg"
}

### Neither grams nor a serving — 400, same rule as when adding
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
}

### An entry that is not in this meal — 404
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries/999999
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "grams": 50
}

### Rename the meal — 200, the entries stay put
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Second breakfast"
}

### Remove one entry — 200 with what is left, regraded
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/entries/1

### Remove a logged recipe — 200. Every entry it unpacked goes, nothing else
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1/recipes/1

### Remove the whole meal — 204
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/meals/1

### The recipe it was logged from is untouched — 200
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1
Accept: application/json
